    1 => [
        'title' => 'The Conceptual Paradigm Shift: Excessive Cognitions and Behaviors',
        'slug' => 'somatic-paradigm-shift-excessive-cognitions',
        'description' => 'The fundamental DSM-5 conceptual revolution: removal of "medically unexplained" requirement and focus on disproportionate cognitive/behavioral reactions.',
        'overview' => "The replacement of DSM-IV 'Somatoform Disorders' with DSM-5 'Somatic Symptom and Related Disorders' represents one of the most radical conceptual revolutions in modern psychiatric nosology. In DSM-IV, diagnoses like Somatization Disorder and Hypochondriasis hinged entirely on a negative medical criterion: somatic symptoms had to be 'medically unexplained' by physical disease. This historical framework created severe clinical problems: it reinforced a false Cartesian mind-body dualism, forced clinicians to prove an untestable negative (that no organic cause existed), stigmatized patients by implying their physical suffering was 'all in their head', and broke down whenever a patient had both a diagnosed medical condition and severe anxiety.\n\nDSM-5 dismantled this flawed paradigm by establishing positive psychological criteria. In DSM-5, whether a somatic symptom has a verified medical etiology is diagnostically irrelevant. An individual can have a documented organic disease (such as rheumatoid arthritis, angina, or cancer) and still qualify for a diagnosis of Somatic Symptom Disorder if their cognitive, affective, and behavioral responses to the symptom are excessive, disproportionate, and distressing.\n\nThis paradigm shift reorients the clinical focus away from searching for elusive organic explanations toward assessing how the patient thinks, feels, and behaves regarding their symptoms. Maladaptive cognitive schemas—such as catastrophic health misinterpretations, bodily hypervigilance, and compulsive health-related checking—are the true drivers of clinical disability and form the primary targets for evidence-based cognitive-behavioral intervention.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "The DSM-5 diagnostic framework for Somatic Symptom Disorder establishes a positive, two-part architecture:\n\nCriterion A requires one or more somatic symptoms that are distressing or result in significant disruption of daily life. (Crucially: The symptoms may or may not be associated with another diagnosed medical condition).\n\nCriterion B requires excessive thoughts, feelings, or behaviors related to the somatic symptoms or associated health concerns, as manifested by at least ONE of the following three markers:\n1. Disproportionate and persistent thoughts about the seriousness of one's symptoms (cognitive domain).\n2. Persistently high level of anxiety about health or symptoms (affective domain).\n3. Excessive time and energy devoted to these symptoms or health concerns (behavioral domain).\n\nCriterion C establishes the chronicity requirement: Although any one somatic symptom may not be continuously present, the state of being symptomatic is persistent (typically more than 6 months).",
        'specifiers' => "Specifier: 'With predominant pain' (assigned if the individual's somatic symptoms predominantly involve pain; replaces the historical DSM-IV Pain Disorder).\n\nSeverity Specifier: Coded as Mild (only 1 Criterion B symptom), Moderate (2 or more Criterion B symptoms), or Severe (2 or more Criterion B symptoms, plus multiple somatic complaints or one very severe symptom).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "DSM-5 Somatic Symptom Disorder vs. DSM-IV Somatization Disorder: DSM-IV required an arbitrary checklist of 8 unexplained symptoms across 4 categories (pain, GI, sexual, pseudoneurological); DSM-5 eliminated this symptom counting, requiring only 1 distressing symptom paired with maladaptive thoughts/behaviors.\n\nSomatic Symptom Disorder vs. Medical Conditions: In medical disease without SSD, the patient's worry is appropriate to medical reality; in SSD, the cognitive anxiety and time expenditure are markedly disproportionate to medical reality.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 48-year-old patient with mild osteoarthritis of the knee confirmed by x-ray. While orthopedic evaluation indicates mild joint wear requiring physical therapy, the patient is completely convinced they will be paralyzed within months (catastrophic cognitive appraisal).\n\nThe patient spends six hours daily researching wheelchairs online, visits four emergency rooms weekly demanding advanced MRIs, and has quit their job to stay in bed (excessive behavioral time expenditure). Despite having an organic medical diagnosis (osteoarthritis), the patient meets full criteria for Somatic Symptom Disorder, Moderate, illustrating that organic disease does not exclude an SSD diagnosis.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: DSM-5 eliminated the requirement that somatic symptoms must be 'medically unexplained'. A patient can have a verified medical condition (e.g., cancer, arthritis) and still be diagnosed with Somatic Symptom Disorder if their thoughts, feelings, or behaviors are disproportionate. Diagnosis requires 1+ distressing somatic symptoms + at least 1 Criterion B psychological feature (thoughts, anxiety, or behaviors) lasting >6 months. Pain Disorder was folded into SSD as the 'With predominant pain' specifier.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Positive Psychological Criteria', 'icon' => '✅', 'definition' => 'Diagnosis is based on the presence of disproportionate thoughts, anxiety, and behaviors rather than the absence of medical explanation.'],
