    2 => [
        'title' => 'Somatic Symptom Disorder (SSD: Distressing Symptoms + High Anxiety)',
        'slug' => 'somatic-symptom-disorder-criteria',
        'description' => 'Diagnostic criteria, single or multiple physical complaints, Criterion B cognitive/behavioral triad, and pain specifier.',
        'overview' => "Somatic Symptom Disorder (SSD) is the central diagnostic entity of the somatic disorders chapter in DSM-5, replacing Somatization Disorder, Undifferentiated Somatoform Disorder, and Pain Disorder. SSD affects approximately 5% to 7% of the general population and up to 15% of patients in primary care, representing a major driver of healthcare utilization, redundant diagnostic testing, and iatrogenic medical harm.\n\nThe clinical presentation of SSD involves one or more persistent physical symptoms—such as localized pain, fatigue, gastrointestinal distress, dizziness, or palpitations—that cause significant disruption in daily life. However, what transforms physical symptoms into a psychiatric disorder is the patient's disproportionate cognitive, affective, and behavioral reaction to those symptoms (Criterion B).\n\nPatients with SSD frequently become caught in a vicious cycle: hypervigilance to bodily sensations amplifies central nervous system perception of somatic discomfort, which triggers intense catastrophic worry ('I have an undiagnosed cancer'), which drives sympathetic autonomic arousal, further worsening physical symptoms and prompting urgent medical visits and diagnostic procedures.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: One or more somatic symptoms that are distressing or result in significant disruption of daily life.\n\nCriterion B: Excessive thoughts, feelings, or behaviors related to the somatic symptoms or associated health concerns, as manifested by at least one of the following:\n1. Disproportionate and persistent thoughts about the seriousness of one's symptoms.\n2. Persistently high level of anxiety about health or symptoms.\n3. Excessive time and energy devoted to these symptoms or health concerns.\n\nCriterion C: Although any one somatic symptom may not be continuously present, the state of being symptomatic is persistent (typically more than 6 months).\n\nDiagnostic Specifiers:\n• 'With predominant pain': For individuals whose somatic symptoms predominantly involve pain (e.g., chronic back pain, fibromyalgia, musculoskeletal pain).\n• 'Persistent': A severe course characterized by severe symptoms, marked impairment, and long duration (more than 6 months).\n• Severity Grading: Mild (1 Criterion B symptom met); Moderate (2 or more Criterion B symptoms met); Severe (2 or more Criterion B symptoms met, plus multiple somatic complaints or one very severe symptom).",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians specify if the condition is 'With predominant pain' and record current severity (Mild, Moderate, Severe) based on the number and intensity of Criterion B psychological features.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "SSD vs. Illness Anxiety Disorder: In Somatic Symptom Disorder, significant somatic symptoms are PROMINENTLY PRESENT; in Illness Anxiety Disorder, somatic symptoms are ABSENT or only minimal, and the individual's primary issue is anxiety about the idea of being sick.\n\nSSD vs. Generalized Anxiety Disorder: In GAD, anxiety is diffuse and shifts across multiple life domains (work, family, finances); in SSD, anxiety is focused persistently on somatic symptoms.\n\nSSD vs. Conversion Disorder: Conversion disorder features specific loss of voluntary motor or sensory function (paralysis, blindness, non-epileptic seizures) with clinical incompatibility; SSD features broader bodily discomfort (pain, fatigue, GI) with excessive health cognitions.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 38-year-old patient who presents with chronic lower abdominal pain and fatigue that began eight months ago. Extensive gastrointestinal workups, colonoscopy, and abdominal imaging are completely normal.\n\nDespite medical reassurance, the patient spends four hours every night researching rare gastrointestinal malignancies, experiences constant terror that physicians are missing a fatal tumor, and has taken medical leave from work to attend appointments with six different gastroenterologists. The presence of distressing physical symptoms for >6 months with catastrophic thoughts, high anxiety, and excessive time expenditure confirms Somatic Symptom Disorder, Severe.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: SSD requires 1+ distressing somatic symptoms + at least 1 Criterion B feature (disproportionate thoughts, high anxiety, excessive time/energy) lasting > 6 months. Physical symptoms are actually present in SSD, distinguishing it from Illness Anxiety Disorder (where physical symptoms are absent or minimal). Pain presentations are coded using the 'With predominant pain' specifier. First-line management involves scheduling regular, predictable clinic visits with a single primary care provider to avoid redundant testing.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Actual Symptom Presence', 'icon' => '🩺', 'definition' => 'Unlike Illness Anxiety Disorder, physical symptoms are prominently present and distressing in SSD.'],
