    3 => [
        'title' => 'Illness Anxiety Disorder (IAD: High Health Anxiety with Minimal Symptoms)',
        'slug' => 'illness-anxiety-disorder-criteria',
        'description' => 'Preoccupation with acquiring or having a serious disease, absence/mildness of somatic symptoms, care-seeking vs. care-avoidant types.',
        'overview' => "Illness Anxiety Disorder (IAD) was established in DSM-5 to capture a specific, historically misunderstood clinical population: individuals who experience severe, disabling anxiety about their health in the complete absence (or only minimal presence) of actual somatic symptoms. In DSM-IV, these individuals were classified under Hypochondriasis; however, research revealed that Hypochondriasis was heterogeneous: roughly 75% of patients had significant physical symptoms (now classified under Somatic Symptom Disorder), while 25% had high health anxiety with minimal or no physical symptoms (now classified under Illness Anxiety Disorder).\n\nThe defining feature of Illness Anxiety Disorder is a persistent preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious, undiagnosed medical illness (such as cancer, HIV, ALS, or cardiovascular disease). If somatic symptoms are present, they are only mild in intensity (such as transient physiological sensations, sinus fullness, or normal muscle twitches), yet the individual interprets them as catastrophic harbingers of terminal disease.\n\nThe individual exhibits an alarm response to health information: hearing about an illness in the news triggers intense anxiety, and the individual either performs excessive health-related checks (checking blood pressure, palpating lymph nodes, examining skin for hours) or engages in maladaptive avoidance (refusing to visit doctors or step foot in hospitals).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: Preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness.\n\nCriterion B: Somatic symptoms are not present or, if present, are only mild in intensity. If another medical condition is present or there is a high risk for developing a medical condition (e.g., strong family history is present), the preoccupation is clearly excessive or disproportionate.\n\nCriterion C: There is a high level of anxiety about health, and the individual is easily alarmed about personal health status.\n\nCriterion D: The individual performs excessive health-related behaviors (e.g., repeatedly checks his or her body for signs of illness) or exhibits maladaptive avoidance (e.g., avoids doctor appointments and hospitals).\n\nCriterion E: Illness preoccupation has been present for at least 6 months, but the specific illness that is feared may change over that period of time.\n\nCriterion F: The illness-related preoccupation is not better explained by another mental disorder (e.g., Somatic Symptom Disorder, Panic Disorder, Generalized Anxiety Disorder, Body Dysmorphic Disorder, Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder, Delusional Disorder somatic type).",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians must specify the behavioral presentation subtype:\n1. 'Care-seeking type': Medical care, including physician visits or undergoing tests and procedures, is frequently used (the classic hypochondriacal presentation).\n2. 'Care-avoidant type': Medical care is rarely used or actively avoided out of intense terror that a doctor will confirm a dreaded terminal diagnosis.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Illness Anxiety Disorder vs. Somatic Symptom Disorder: This is the single most tested board distinction. In Somatic Symptom Disorder, somatic symptoms are PROMINENTLY PRESENT and distressing; in Illness Anxiety Disorder, somatic symptoms are ABSENT or only minimal.\n\nIAD vs. Panic Disorder: In Panic Disorder, the individual fears immediate death during acute panic surges (e.g., having a heart attack right now); in IAD, the fear is of having a chronic, insidious disease (e.g., developing cancer or ALS).\n\nIAD vs. Delusional Disorder, Somatic Type: In IAD, the individual acknowledges the possibility that the disease may not be present (insight is preserved or fluctuates); in Delusional Disorder, the belief of disease is fixed with 100% unshakeable certainty.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 32-year-old lawyer who has had an intense fear of developing pancreatic cancer for the past seven months. The lawyer has no abdominal pain, no weight loss, and no physical symptoms whatsoever.\n\nHowever, upon noticing normal stomach gurgling after meals, the lawyer experiences overwhelming panic, spends four hours daily reading oncology journals, inspects their stool twice daily, and has visited three different oncologists demanding abdominal CT scans (which were completely clear). Meeting criteria for health preoccupation with minimal physical symptoms for >6 months and excessive health checking, the clinical formulation confirms Illness Anxiety Disorder, Care-seeking type.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Illness Anxiety Disorder involves preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness with ABSENT or MINIMAL somatic symptoms (distinguishing it from Somatic Symptom Disorder). Duration must be at least 6 months. The two subtypes are: Care-seeking type (frequent doctor visits/tests) and Care-avoidant type (avoids doctors out of fear). Replaced the non-somatic portion of DSM-IV Hypochondriasis. Reassurance from normal test results provides only brief relief before anxiety shifts to a new disease.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Absence of Significant Somatic Symptoms', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'The definitive diagnostic distinction: somatic symptoms are absent or only mild/transient.'],
            ['title' => 'Care-Seeking vs. Care-Avoidant Subtypes', 'icon' => '↔️', 'definition' => 'Care-seeking frequently utilizes medical testing; Care-avoidant avoids doctors out of fear of confirmation.'],
            ['title' => 'The 6-Month Chronicity Rule', 'icon' => '🗓️', 'definition' => 'Health preoccupation must endure for at least 6 months, although the specific feared disease may shift.'],
            ['title' => 'Transient Relief from Reassurance', 'icon' => '🩺', 'definition' => 'Normal lab/imaging results temporarily alleviate anxiety before doubt resurfaces, perpetuating testing cycles.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Care-Seeking Type', 'definition' => 'A behavioral presentation of Illness Anxiety Disorder characterized by frequent medical visits, second opinions, and diagnostic imaging.'],
            ['term' => 'Care-Avoidant Type', 'definition' => 'A behavioral presentation of Illness Anxiety Disorder characterized by avoidance of physicians and hospitals out of terror of receiving a fatal diagnosis.'],
            ['term' => 'Hypochondriasis', 'definition' => 'The historical DSM-IV diagnostic term, retired and divided in DSM-5 between Somatic Symptom Disorder (75%) and Illness Anxiety Disorder (25%).'],
            ['term' => 'Body Checking', 'definition' => 'Compulsive physical examinations (measuring lymph nodes, inspecting skin, checking pulse) performed to detect early signs of disease.']
        ]
