    4 => [
        'title' => 'Conversion Disorder (Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder)',
        'slug' => 'conversion-disorder-functional-neurology',
        'description' => 'Altered voluntary motor or sensory function, clinical incompatibility with neurological disease, positive signs (Hoover\'s sign), and la belle indifférence.',
        'overview' => "Conversion Disorder (Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder) is characterized by one or more symptoms of altered voluntary motor or sensory function that are clinically incompatible with recognized neurological or medical conditions. Historically rooted in psychoanalytic concepts of 'hysteria'—wherein unmanageable psychological conflicts were thought to be unconsciously 'converted' into physical neurological deficits—DSM-5 substantially modernized the diagnostic criteria.\n\nA major conceptual advancement in DSM-5 was the elimination of the historical requirement that psychological stressors must precede symptom onset. In DSM-IV, clinicians were required to identify an acute psychological stressor to diagnose conversion disorder. DSM-5 removed this requirement because extensive clinical evidence demonstrated that psychological stressors cannot always be identified at onset, and requiring psychological causation led to delayed diagnoses and misattributions.\n\nCrucially, DSM-5 mandated positive evidence of neurological incompatibility. A diagnosis of Conversion Disorder cannot be made simply because medical tests are 'normal'; instead, physical examination must demonstrate explicit positive clinical signs showing incompatibility with known neuroanatomy (e.g., Hoover's sign in leg weakness, preserved pupillary light reflexes in functional blindness, or closed eyes with active resistance during non-epileptic seizures).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: One or more symptoms of altered voluntary motor or sensory function.\n\nCriterion B: Clinical findings provide evidence of incompatibility between the symptom and recognized neurological or medical conditions. (Note: This requires specific physical examination maneuvers demonstrating positive signs of functional etiology, rather than simple test negativity).\n\nCriterion C: The symptom or deficit is not better explained by another medical or mental disorder.\n\nCriterion D: The symptom or deficit causes clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning, or warrants medical evaluation.\n\nClinical Symptom Subtypes:\n• With weakness or paralysis.\n• With abnormal movement (e.g., tremor, dystonia, chorea, gait disorder).\n• With swallowing symptoms (globus sensation).\n• With speech symptoms (dysphonia, slurred speech).\n• With attacks or seizures (psychogenic non-epileptic seizures [PNES]).\n• With anesthesia or sensory loss (e.g., glove-and-stocking loss with sharp anatomical cutoff).\n• With special sensory symptom (e.g., functional blindness, tunnel vision, functional deafness).\n• With mixed symptoms.",
        'specifiers' => "Specifier: 'Acute episode' (symptoms present for less than 6 months) or 'Persistent' (symptoms present for 6 months or more).\n\nSpecifier: 'With psychological stressor' (specifying the stressor if identified) or 'Without psychological stressor'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Conversion Disorder vs. Malingering: In Conversion Disorder, the symptom is involuntary and experienced as completely real by the patient; in Malingering, the symptom is consciously and intentionally fabricated for obvious external incentives (legal, financial, military avoidance).\n\nConversion Disorder vs. Factitious Disorder: In Factitious Disorder, the symptom is consciously fabricated to adopt the sick role without external incentive; in Conversion, the symptom is involuntary.\n\nConversion vs. True Neurological Disease: True neurological diseases follow anatomical dermatomes and pathways; conversion symptoms violate neuroanatomy (e.g., functional hemiplegia showing positive Hoover's sign).",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 26-year-old individual who awoke with sudden total paralysis of the right leg. Neurological examination reveals normal reflexes and no Babinski sign. The neurologist performs Hoover's test:\n\nWhen the patient is asked to flex the normal left hip against resistance, the paralyzed right heel involuntarily presses downward firmly into the examiner's hand (positive Hoover's sign), demonstrating intact involuntary motor pathways incompatible with structural hemiplegia. The positive neurological incompatibility confirms Conversion Disorder (Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder), With weakness or paralysis, Acute episode.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Conversion Disorder requires 1+ symptoms of altered voluntary motor or sensory function with POSITIVE clinical evidence of incompatibility with neurological disease. DSM-5 eliminated the requirement of an antecedent psychological stressor. Hoover's sign demonstrates functional leg weakness: downward pressure of the weak heel during contralateral hip flexion confirms intact motor pathways. La belle indifférence (bizarre lack of concern about severe disability) is historically associated with conversion, but has poor diagnostic specificity.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Positive Neurological Incompatibility', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Requires objective physical exam signs demonstrating inconsistency with known neuroanatomy (e.g., Hoover\'s sign).'],
            ['title' => 'Elimination of Stressor Requirement', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'DSM-5 removed the mandatory requirement of antecedent psychological trauma to establish diagnosis.'],
            ['title' => 'Involuntary Phenomenological Nature', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'Symptoms are genuinely experienced and not consciously or intentionally produced by the patient.'],
            ['title' => 'Hoover\'s Sign & Clinical Maneuvers', 'icon' => '🦶', 'definition' => 'Objective bedside tests demonstrating preserved involuntary motor pathways during contralateral movement.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Hoover\'s Sign', 'definition' => 'A physical examination sign in functional leg weakness where involuntary extension of the paretic leg occurs when flexing the contralateral normal hip against resistance.'],
            ['term' => 'Psychogenic Non-Epileptic Seizures (PNES)', 'definition' => 'Paroxysmal episodes resembling epileptic seizures that lack ictal epileptiform EEG activity, classically presenting with forced eye closure and pelvic thrusting.'],
            ['term' => 'La Belle Indifférence', 'definition' => 'A historical clinical phenomenon characterized by a curious lack of emotional concern or distress regarding a severe physical disability.'],
            ['term' => 'Glove-and-Stocking Anesthesia', 'definition' => 'A sensory deficit terminating abruptly at the wrist or ankle, violating dermatomal neurological pathways, characteristic of functional sensory conversion.']
        ]
