    5 => [
        'title' => 'Psychological Factors Affecting Other Medical Conditions',
        'slug' => 'psychological-factors-affecting-medical-conditions',
        'description' => 'The presence of an actual medical condition, adverse psychological influence on course/treatment, and health risk escalation.',
        'overview' => "Psychological Factors Affecting Other Medical Conditions (PFAOMC) was relocated in DSM-5 from the 'Other Conditions That May Be a Focus of Clinical Attention' section and established as a formal, codable mental disorder within Somatic Symptom and Related Disorders. This classification emphasizes the profound clinical reality that psychological and behavioral factors can directly alter the physiological course, treatment adherence, and medical outcomes of diagnosed physical illnesses.\n\nUnlike Somatic Symptom Disorder—where the core problem is excessive anxiety about physical symptoms—PFAOMC requires the documented presence of an actual, established general medical condition (such as diabetes mellitus, cardiovascular disease, severe asthma, chronic kidney disease, or peptic ulcer disease). The psychiatric pathology lies in how psychological or behavioral factors adversely affect that medical condition.\n\nMechanisms of adverse impact include: (1) psychological factors directly altering the underlying pathophysiology (e.g., stress-induced vasoconstriction precipitating Takotsubo cardiomyopathy or coronary vasospasm); (2) behavioral factors interfering with treatment or medication adherence (e.g., a diabetic patient omitting insulin injections to manipulate body weight); or (3) psychological traits exacerbating health risks (e.g., severe Type A hostility accelerating coronary atherosclerosis).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: A medical symptom or condition (other than a mental disorder) is present.\n\nCriterion B: Psychological or behavioral factors adversely affect the medical condition in one of the following ways:\n1. The factors have influenced the course of the medical condition as shown by a close temporal association between the psychological factors and the development or exacerbation of, or delayed recovery from, the medical condition.\n2. The factors interfere with the treatment of the medical condition (e.g., poor adherence).\n3. The factors constitute additional well-established health risks for the individual.\n4. The factors influence the underlying pathophysiology, precipitating or exacerbating symptoms or necessitating medical attention.\n\nCriterion C: The psychological and behavioral factors in Criterion B are not better explained by another mental disorder (e.g., panic disorder, major depressive disorder, somatic symptom disorder).\n\nSeverity Specifiers:\n• Mild: Increases medical risk (e.g., inconsistent adherence with antihypertensive medications).\n• Moderate: Aggravates underlying medical condition (e.g., anxiety worsening asthma exacerbations).\n• Severe: Results in medical hospitalization or emergency room visit.\n• Extreme: Results in severe, life-threatening risk (e.g., ignoring heart attack symptoms).",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians must record the specific diagnosed medical condition (e.g., Psychological Factors Affecting Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus) and specify severity (Mild, Moderate, Severe, Extreme) based on clinical medical risk.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "PFAOMC vs. Somatic Symptom Disorder: In PFAOMC, psychological factors adversely affect an ACTUAL medical condition; in SSD, the focus is on disproportionate thoughts and anxiety about symptoms regardless of medical status.\n\nPFAOMC vs. Adjustment Disorder: In Adjustment Disorder, emotional symptoms develop IN RESPONSE TO receiving a medical diagnosis; in PFAOMC, psychological factors ADVERSELY INFLUENCE the medical condition itself.\n\nPFAOMC vs. Major Depressive Disorder: If medication non-adherence is secondary to severe depressive anhedonia and psychomotor retardation, MDD is coded as the primary diagnosis.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 45-year-old patient with severe, brittle asthma who is in acute denial about having a chronic respiratory illness. Whenever acute wheezing develops, the patient refuses to take inhaled corticosteroids, asserting that 'breathing exercises are all I need.'\n\nOver the past six months, this behavioral refusal has resulted in three ICU admissions for status asthmaticus requiring mechanical intubation. The patient does not meet criteria for major depression or somatic symptom disorder. The adverse behavioral interference resulting in life-threatening medical hospitalization confirms Psychological Factors Affecting Other Medical Conditions (Asthma), Severe.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: PFAOMC requires the documented presence of an actual medical condition (e.g., asthma, diabetes, hypertension). Psychological or behavioral factors adversely affect the medical condition by influencing course, interfering with treatment, creating health risks, or altering pathophysiology. Severity is graded based on medical risk (Mild, Moderate, Severe, Extreme; e.g., ICU admission = Severe). Differentiate from Adjustment Disorder (where emotions are a reaction to diagnosis).",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Documented Physical Disease Mandatory', 'icon' => '🩺', 'definition' => 'Requires the confirmed presence of a general medical condition (e.g., diabetes, asthma, heart disease).'],
            ['title' => 'Four Adverse Impact Pathways', 'icon' => '📉', 'definition' => 'Influencing course, interfering with treatment adherence, adding health risks, or triggering pathophysiology.'],
            ['title' => 'Graded Medical Risk Severity', 'icon' => '🚨', 'definition' => 'Severity reflects medical outcome risk: Mild (risk), Moderate (aggravates), Severe (hospitalization), Extreme (life-threatening).'],
            ['title' => 'Directionality Principle', 'icon' => '↔️', 'definition' => 'Psychology impacts medical course (PFAOMC), rather than medical diagnosis triggering emotional distress (Adjustment).']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Psychological Factors Affecting Other Medical Conditions', 'definition' => 'A formal DSM-5 diagnosis where psychological or behavioral factors adversely alter the course or treatment of a physical illness.'],
            ['term' => 'Takotsubo Cardiomyopathy', 'definition' => 'A temporary heart condition characterized by acute ventricular ballooning triggered by severe emotional stress, exemplifying psychophysiological impact.'],
            ['term' => 'Treatment Non-Adherence', 'definition' => 'The failure or refusal to follow prescribed medical regimens (medications, dietary restrictions, therapies) due to psychological factors.'],
            ['term' => 'Diabulimia', 'definition' => 'A colloquial term for an extreme behavioral pattern in Type 1 Diabetes where insulin is deliberately restricted to induce rapid weight loss.']
        ]
