    6 => [
        'title' => 'Factitious Disorder: Imposed on Self & Imposed on Another (Munchausen)',
        'slug' => 'factitious-disorder-self-another-munchausen',
        'description' => 'Falsification of physical or psychological signs, deceptive induction of injury, absence of external incentives, and Munchausen by proxy.',
        'overview' => "Factitious Disorder is characterized by the deliberate, intentional falsification of medical or psychological signs or symptoms, or the induction of injury or disease, associated with identified deception. Individuals with Factitious Disorder intentionally feign illness, simulate symptoms, contaminate laboratory samples, or physically harm themselves in order to present themselves (or another person) as ill, impaired, or injured.\n\nA fundamental, high-yield diagnostic distinction in DSM-5 is the motivation: in Factitious Disorder, the deceptive behavior is present even in the ABSENCE of obvious external rewards or incentives. The primary psychological motivation is internal: the compulsive psychological drive to adopt the 'sick role', obtain medical attention, and be cared for by clinical personnel.\n\nThis crucial criterion separates Factitious Disorder from Malingering. In Malingering, symptoms are also intentionally fabricated; however, Malingering is driven by obvious external incentives (e.g., avoiding military service, escaping criminal prosecution, obtaining narcotic drugs, or securing financial disability benefits). DSM-5 recognizes two variants: Factitious Disorder Imposed on Self (historically Munchausen syndrome) and Factitious Disorder Imposed on Another (historically Munchausen syndrome by proxy).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "For Factitious Disorder Imposed on Self:\nCriterion A: Falsification of physical or psychological signs or symptoms, or induction of injury or disease, associated with identified deception.\nCriterion B: The individual presents himself or herself to others as ill, impaired, or injured.\nCriterion C: The deceptive behavior is evident even in the absence of obvious external rewards (e.g., economic gain, avoiding legal duties).\nCriterion D: The behavior is not better explained by another mental disorder, such as delusional disorder or another psychotic disorder.\n\nFor Factitious Disorder Imposed on Another (Munchausen by Proxy):\nCriterion A: Falsification of physical or psychological signs or symptoms, or induction of injury or disease, in another, associated with identified deception.\nCriterion B: The individual presents another individual (victim) to others as ill, impaired, or injured.\nCriterion C: The deceptive behavior is evident even in the absence of obvious external rewards.\nCriterion D: The behavior is not better explained by another mental disorder.\n(CRITICAL CODING RULE: The perpetrator, NOT the victim, receives this diagnosis; the victim may be assigned an abuse V-code/Z-code).",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians specify episode pattern: 'Single episode' or 'Recurrent episodes' (the classic Munchausen pattern involving repeated hospitalizations across multiple medical centers).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Factitious Disorder vs. Malingering: The definitive board distinction. In Factitious Disorder, motivation is internal (adopting the sick role) without external reward; in Malingering, motivation is driven by obvious external incentives (money, avoiding jail, drugs).\n\nFactitious Disorder vs. Somatic Symptom Disorder: In SSD, physical symptoms are genuine and not intentionally produced; in Factitious Disorder, symptoms are intentionally and deceptively produced or fabricated.\n\nFactitious Disorder vs. Conversion Disorder: In Conversion, symptoms are involuntary and not feigned; in Factitious, symptoms are intentionally falsified.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 29-year-old nurse who presents to multiple emergency rooms with recurrent, severe polymicrobial sepsis and skin abscesses. Blood cultures grow fecal bacteria (E. coli, Bacteroides).\n\nHospital security cameras record the patient surreptitiously injecting fecal slurry into their central venous catheter line in the bathroom. The patient has no pending lawsuits, no financial insurance payouts, and refuses pain medications, expressing only a desire to be cared for by doctors. The intentional induction of disease with deception in the absence of external incentives confirms Factitious Disorder Imposed on Self, Recurrent episodes.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Factitious Disorder involves intentional falsification or induction of disease with identified deception in the ABSENCE of external incentives (motivation is the internal 'sick role'). Malingering involves intentional fabrication for OBVIOUS EXTERNAL INCENTIVES (money, avoiding jail, drugs; not a mental disorder). In Factitious Disorder Imposed on Another (Munchausen by proxy), the PERPETRATOR receives the diagnosis, not the victim. Common methods: insulin injection, lab contamination, adding blood to urine.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Absence of External Incentives Rule', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'The definitive test separating Factitious Disorder (internal sick role) from Malingering (external gain).'],
            ['title' => 'Identified Deceptive Fabrication', 'icon' => '🎭', 'definition' => 'Requires objective evidence of deliberate symptom feigning, lab contamination, or covert injury induction.'],
            ['title' => 'Imposed on Another Coding Mandate', 'icon' => '👶', 'definition' => 'The diagnosis is assigned strictly to the abusive caregiver perpetrator, never to the pediatric or dependent victim.'],
            ['title' => 'Classic Munchausen Wanderlust', 'icon' => '🏥', 'definition' => 'Recurrent presentations at diverse hospitals under aliases when clinical deception is suspected by staff.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Sick Role', 'definition' => 'The social and psychological role wherein a person receives medical attention, sympathy, and exemption from normal responsibilities.'],
            ['term' => 'Munchausen Syndrome by Proxy', 'definition' => 'The historical eponym for Factitious Disorder Imposed on Another, recognized as a severe form of child or dependent medical abuse.'],
            ['term' => 'Malingering', 'definition' => 'The intentional production of false or grossly exaggerated physical or psychological symptoms motivated by external incentives.'],
            ['term' => 'Pseudologia Fantastica', 'definition' => 'A pattern of elaborate, dramatic, and plausible fabrications or untruths frequently told by individuals with severe factitious disorder.']
        ]
    ],
    7 => [
        'title' => 'Central Sensitization, Neurological Incompatibility, & Secondary Gain',
        'slug' => 'central-sensitization-neurological-incompatibility',
        'description' => 'Neurobiological mechanisms of somatic amplification, objective positive physical signs, and primary vs. secondary gain.',
        'overview' => "Understanding the modern pathophysiological and psychological mechanisms underlying somatic disorders requires integrating contemporary neurobiology with clinical phenomenology. Decades of functional neuroimaging and neurophysiological research have discredited the simplistic notion that somatic disorders are purely 'psychological' or 'feigned', revealing complex alterations in central sensory processing and motor inhibition.\n\nCentral sensitization represents a key biological mechanism in Somatic Symptom Disorder and chronic functional pain syndromes (such as fibromyalgia). Under prolonged stress or peripheral inflammation, spinal dorsal horn neurons and thalamocortical pain networks undergo neuroplastic changes characterized by hyperexcitability, lowered firing thresholds, and expanded receptive fields. As a consequence, normal non-noxious sensory input is amplified into conscious pain (allodynia) and noxious stimuli produce exaggerated pain (hyperalgesia).\n\nIn Conversion Disorder, functional MRI studies reveal abnormal functional connectivity between the limbic system (amygdala and insula) and motor planning regions (supplementary motor area), demonstrating that emotional distress unconsciously inhibits voluntary motor execution. Furthermore, psychoanalytic concepts of primary gain (internal reduction of anxiety) and secondary gain (external interpersonal sympathy and relief from responsibilities) provide clinical context for symptom maintenance.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Evaluating Somatic Pathophysiology and Clinical Signs:\n\n1. Central Sensitization: Documenting widespread pain amplification, visceral hyperalgesia, and autonomic dysregulation across chronic somatic presentations.\n\n2. Positive Neurological Incompatibility Signs in Conversion Disorder:\n• Hoover's Sign: Involuntary extension of the paretic leg during contralateral hip flexion against resistance.\n• Abductor Sign: Contralateral leg abduction during resisted abduction of the normal leg.\n• Non-Epileptic Seizure Signs: Resistance to eyelid opening, preserved pupillary light reflexes during ictal state, asynchronous thrashing, absence of post-ictal lactic acidosis, and normal ictal EEG.\n• Tubular (Tunnel) Vision: Visual field remains identical in size regardless of distance from testing screen (violating optical physics).\n\n3. Gain Analysis: Differentiating internal primary gain from external secondary gain.",
        'specifiers' => "Documenting physical exam maneuvers and objective physiological testing confirming positive neurological incompatibility.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Central Sensitization vs. Peripheral Neuropathy: Peripheral neuropathy follows discrete dermatomal or peripheral nerve distributions with objective nerve conduction velocity slowing; central sensitization produces non-dermatomal, widespread amplification.\n\nPNES vs. Frontal Lobe Epilepsy: Frontal lobe nocturnal seizures can feature hypermotor movements; continuous video-EEG monitoring is the gold-standard diagnostic tool.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 34-year-old patient admitted for apparent bilateral blindness following an acute interpersonal crisis. Examination reveals normal optic discs, normal visual evoked potentials, and intact pupillary constriction to light.\n\nWhen the examiner moves a large striped rotating drum in front of the patient's eyes, optokinetic nystagmus is clearly elicited. Because optokinetic nystagmus requires intact cortical visual tracking, the sign demonstrates positive neurological incompatibility with true cortical blindness, confirming Conversion Disorder, With special sensory symptom.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Central sensitization involves hyperexcitability of central pain processing circuits leading to hyperalgesia and allodynia. Positive signs of conversion: Hoover's sign (weak leg involuntarily presses down during contralateral hip flexion), optokinetic nystagmus in functional blindness, and closed eyes with resistance in PNES. Primary gain is the internal relief of psychic anxiety; secondary gain is external interpersonal sympathy or release from obligations. Video-EEG is gold standard for ruling out PNES.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Central Sensitization Dynamics', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Neuroplastic hyperexcitability in central nociceptive circuits amplifying non-noxious sensory stimuli into conscious pain.'],
            ['title' => 'Positive Incompatibility Testing', 'icon' => '🦶', 'definition' => 'Objective bedside maneuvers (Hoover\'s test, optokinetic drum) proving functional motor/sensory integrity.'],
            ['title' => 'Primary vs. Secondary Gain', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Primary gain is unconscious internal anxiety reduction; secondary gain is external interpersonal support and relief from duties.'],
            ['title' => 'Video-EEG Diagnostic Gold Standard', 'icon' => '📹', 'definition' => 'Continuous video-EEG monitoring definitively distinguishes non-epileptic seizures from true epileptic discharges.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Allodynia', 'definition' => 'Pain resulting from a stimulus (such as light touch or clothing) that does not normally provoke pain, a hallmark of central sensitization.'],
            ['term' => 'Hyperalgesia', 'definition' => 'An abnormally increased sensitivity and exaggerated response to a painful stimulus.'],
            ['term' => 'Optokinetic Nystagmus (OKN)', 'definition' => 'An involuntary physiological eye movement elicited by tracking moving visual patterns, proving intact visual pathway function.'],
            ['term' => 'Secondary Gain', 'definition' => 'Interpersonal, social, or practical benefits (attention, care, exemption from work) accrued as a secondary consequence of being ill.']
        ]
    ],
    8 => [
        'title' => 'Differential Diagnosis: SSD vs. Organic Disease vs. Conversion vs. Factitious vs. Malingering',
        'slug' => 'differential-diagnosis-somatic-spectrum-malingering',
        'description' => 'Definitive differential matrix across the somatic spectrum: intentionality of production, presence of external incentives, and clinical signs.',
        'overview' => "The differential diagnosis of somatic symptom presentations represents one of the most critical legal, ethical, and medical responsibilities in clinical practice. Because a misstep can result in either subjecting a genuinely ill patient to psychiatric dismissal or enabling massive healthcare fraud and dangerous invasive procedures, clinicians must command a rigorous, systematic diagnostic framework.\n\nThe foundational differential matrix across somatic presentations is governed by two orthogonal dimensions: (1) Is the symptom production voluntary (conscious/intentional) or involuntary (unconscious/genuine)? and (2) What is the underlying motivation or incentive?\n\nIf symptoms are involuntary and genuine: Does the presentation feature altered voluntary motor/sensory function with positive incompatibility? -> Conversion Disorder. Does it feature distressing bodily symptoms with excessive thoughts/anxiety? -> Somatic Symptom Disorder. Does it feature health anxiety with minimal physical symptoms? -> Illness Anxiety Disorder. Does an actual medical disease exist whose course is worsened by psychology? -> Psychological Factors Affecting Other Medical Conditions.\n\nIf symptoms are voluntary (consciously fabricated): Is motivation internal to adopt the sick role without external reward? -> Factitious Disorder. Is motivation driven by obvious external incentives (money, avoiding criminal charges)? -> Malingering.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "The Definitive Somatic Differential Matrix:\n\n1. Somatic Symptom Disorder:\n• Production: Involuntary / Genuine.\n• Physical Symptoms: Present & Distressing.\n• Core Feature: Disproportionate thoughts, high anxiety, excessive time/energy.\n\n2. Illness Anxiety Disorder:\n• Production: Involuntary / Genuine.\n• Physical Symptoms: Absent or Minimal.\n• Core Feature: Preoccupation with having/acquiring serious disease.\n\n3. Conversion Disorder:\n• Production: Involuntary / Genuine.\n• Physical Symptoms: Neurological motor/sensory deficit.\n• Core Feature: Positive clinical evidence of incompatibility with neuroanatomy.\n\n4. Factitious Disorder:\n• Production: Voluntary / Consciously Fabricated with Deception.\n• Motivation: Internal (adopting the 'sick role', medical attention).\n• External Incentive: ABSENT.\n\n5. Malingering (Not a Mental Disorder):\n• Production: Voluntary / Consciously Fabricated.\n• Motivation: External Incentives (financial disability, evading military/prison, obtaining drugs).\n• External Incentive: PRESENT & OBVIOUS.",
        'specifiers' => "Formulations must explicitly record: (1) Primary psychiatric diagnosis; (2) Degree of intentionality and deception; and (3) Documented presence of external incentives.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Medical Illness vs. Somatic Disorders: Systemic lupus, multiple sclerosis, acute intermittent porphyria, and endocrine disorders can present with fluctuating non-dermatomal symptoms; complete medical workups are mandatory.\n\nMalingering vs. Factitious Disorder: Malingering is NOT a mental disorder in DSM-5 (coded as a V-code); Factitious Disorder is a formal psychiatric disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 35-year-old construction worker facing imminent termination who presents claiming complete bilateral leg paralysis following a minor slip on the job. The worker has filed a $500,000 workers' compensation lawsuit and demands full permanent disability benefits.\n\nCovert surveillance video recorded by investigators shows the worker briskly jogging across a supermarket parking lot, lifting heavy groceries into their truck. The intentional fabrication of symptoms motivated by obvious external financial incentives confirms Malingering (V65.2), ruling out Conversion and Factitious Disorder.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Memorize the intentionality and incentive matrix: Genuine symptoms = SSD, IAD, Conversion. Fabricated symptoms + NO external incentive (sick role) = Factitious Disorder. Fabricated symptoms + OBVIOUS EXTERNAL INCENTIVE (money, avoiding jail) = Malingering. Malingering is NOT a mental disorder (V-code). Hoover's sign confirms Conversion. Absence/minimal symptoms confirms Illness Anxiety Disorder.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The Somatic Diagnostic Matrix', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Two-dimensional matrix: Intentional vs. Involuntary production, crossed with Internal vs. External motivation.'],
            ['title' => 'Malingering as Non-Psychiatric V-Code', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Malingering represents an intentional, motivated behavioral strategy for external gain, not an Axis I mental illness.'],
            ['title' => 'The Sick Role Distinction', 'icon' => '🏥', 'definition' => 'Factitious disorder is motivated by the internal psychological need to be cared for as a patient, lacking financial motives.'],
            ['title' => 'Objective Video Surveillance Evidence', 'icon' => '📹', 'definition' => 'Inconsistent motor functioning outside clinical settings definitively distinguishes malingering from neurological disease.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Malingering', 'definition' => 'The intentional production of false or grossly exaggerated physical or psychological symptoms, motivated by external incentives.'],
            ['term' => 'V-Code / Z-Code', 'definition' => 'A diagnostic coding category in medical classification used for conditions that are a focus of clinical attention but not mental disorders.'],
            ['term' => 'Conscious Intentionality', 'definition' => 'The deliberate, voluntary cognitive execution of symptom feigning or deception, present in Factitious Disorder and Malingering.'],
            ['term' => 'Involuntary Production', 'definition' => 'Somatic symptoms experienced by the patient as completely genuine and outside conscious motor control, present in SSD and Conversion.']
        ]
    ],
    9 => [
        'title' => 'Educational Case Studies: Navigating Diagnostic Pitfalls',
        'slug' => 'educational-case-studies-somatic-disorders',
        'description' => 'Clinical formulations, primary care management, avoiding iatrogenic harm, and high-yield board question dissection.',
        'overview' => "Clinical competency in Somatic Symptom and Related Disorders requires navigating high-stakes diagnostic pitfalls and countertransference challenges. Patients with somatic disorders are among the most frustrating and challenging for medical teams: redundant diagnostic investigations, conflicting second opinions, and patient demands for invasive procedures frequently provoke physician irritation, leading to either hostile abandonment or dangerous, collusive polypharmacy.\n\nEvidence-based clinical management requires a proactive, biopsychosocial primary care model. Best clinical practice establishes scheduled, regular, predictable clinic appointments (e.g., every 4 to 6 weeks for 15 minutes) with a single primary care provider, regardless of whether the patient is experiencing acute symptoms. This breaks the conditioning loop where medical attention is contingent upon acute symptom complaints, reduces emergency department utilization, and prevents redundant invasive testing.\n\nThis educational module analyzes multi-layered case studies simulating complex hospital consultations and board examination stems, demonstrating how to systematically apply DSM-5 criteria, assign accurate diagnoses, and construct compassionate, safe management plans.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Case Formulation Methodology:\n1. Step 1: Medical Verification. Are symptoms genuine or fabricated? If genuine -> Evaluate for SSD, IAD, Conversion, or PFAOMC. If fabricated -> Evaluate for Factitious Disorder vs. Malingering.\n2. Step 2: Symptom Characteristic Analysis. Are symptoms predominantly neurological (Conversion)? Are physical symptoms absent/minimal (IAD)? Are physical symptoms present with high anxiety (SSD)?\n3. Step 3: Duration Verification. Verify 6-month chronicity for SSD, IAD, and PFAOMC.\n4. Step 4: Management Formulation. Establish the scheduled primary care protocol.",
        'specifiers' => "Diagnostic formulation must record primary somatic diagnoses, clinical specifiers ('With predominant pain', 'Care-seeking type'), and management recommendations.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Case analyses systematically contrast: SSD vs. IAD; Conversion Disorder vs. Multiple Sclerosis; Factitious Disorder vs. Malingering; and PFAOMC vs. Major Depressive Disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "Educational Case Vignette: A 42-year-old patient presents with six months of chronic abdominal bloating and fatigue. Extensive gastroenterological testing is normal. The patient visits emergency rooms twice weekly, spends four hours daily checking their abdomen, and reports intense terror of dying from cancer.\n\nClinical formulation identifies Somatic Symptom Disorder, Severe. The consulting psychiatrist implements the evidence-based management protocol: establishing scheduled 15-minute visits every month with one primary physician, performing focused physical exams without ordering redundant imaging, and initiating Cognitive Behavioral Therapy to address catastrophic health cognitions.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: The cornerstone of management for Somatic Symptom Disorder is scheduled, regular appointments with a single primary care provider. Avoid redundant diagnostic testing and invasive procedures to prevent iatrogenic harm. Reassure the patient that physical symptoms are real while redirecting focus to functional coping. Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) is the gold-standard psychotherapy for somatic symptom amplification.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Scheduled Primary Care Model', 'icon' => '📅', 'definition' => 'Scheduling predictable regular visits breaks the behavioral reinforcement loop linking medical attention to acute symptom flares.'],
            ['title' => 'Single-Provider Gatekeeper Strategy', 'icon' => '🩺', 'definition' => 'Designating one primary physician to coordinate all medical care eliminates doctor shopping and conflicting orders.'],
            ['title' => 'Prevention of Iatrogenic Complications', 'icon' => '🛡️', 'definition' => 'Resisting patient demands for unnecessary exploratory surgeries, invasive biopsies, and addictive analgesics.'],
            ['title' => 'CBT for Somatic Amplification', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'Cognitive-behavioral restructuring targeting catastrophic misinterpretations, bodily checking, and avoidance behaviors.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Gatekeeper Model', 'definition' => 'A healthcare management strategy where a primary care clinician controls and coordinates all specialist referrals and diagnostic testing.'],
            ['term' => 'Countertransference', 'definition' => 'The clinician\'s emotional reaction to a patient, classically manifesting as frustration, helplessness, or irritation in somatic disorders.'],
            ['term' => 'Somatization', 'definition' => 'The expression of psychological distress and interpersonal conflict through somatic physical symptoms.'],
            ['term' => 'Validation Protocol', 'definition' => 'A clinical communication strategy where the physician validates that the patient\'s physical suffering is genuine before addressing psychological coping.']
        ]
    ],
    10 => [
        'title' => 'Chapter Mastery & Board-Exam Synthesis',
        'slug' => 'chapter-mastery-board-exam-synthesis-somatic',
        'description' => 'Comprehensive synthesis of Somatic Symptom and Related Disorders, diagnostic criteria, and high-yield board pearls.',
        'overview' => "This capstone unit delivers an advanced synthesis of Somatic Symptom and Related Disorders under DSM-5 criteria, consolidating core diagnostic formulations, duration thresholds, and high-yield board pearls for professional licensure examinations. Somatic pathology represents heavily tested content across clinical examinations, demanding exact command over diagnostic criteria, specifiers, and differential boundaries.\n\nMastery requires commanding the clinical distinctions separating Somatic Symptom Disorder, Illness Anxiety Disorder, Conversion Disorder, Psychological Factors Affecting Other Medical Conditions, Factitious Disorder, and Malingering. Furthermore, candidates must navigate DSM-5 structural refinements, including the removal of the 'medically unexplained' requirement, the positive Criterion B psychological criteria, the separation of IAD from SSD, and the positive evidence of incompatibility mandate in Conversion Disorder.\n\nBy synthesizing clinical vignettes, diagnostic decision matrices, and evidence-based neurobiological mechanisms, this unit prepares students and clinicians for board examination excellence and compassionate clinical practice.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Mastery Summary of Core Somatic Diagnostic Rules:\n\n1. Somatic Symptom Disorder: 1+ distressing somatic symptoms + >= 1 Criterion B psychological feature (disproportionate thoughts, high anxiety, excessive time/energy); duration > 6 months. Specifier: 'With predominant pain'. Coexists with medical disease.\n\n2. Illness Anxiety Disorder: Preoccupation with having/acquiring serious illness; somatic symptoms ABSENT or only minimal; high health anxiety; excessive checking or avoidance; duration >= 6 months. Subtypes: Care-seeking vs. Care-avoidant.\n\n3. Conversion Disorder: Altered voluntary motor or sensory function; POSITIVE clinical evidence of incompatibility with neuroanatomy (e.g., Hoover's sign); psychological stressor NOT required; involuntary.\n\n4. Psychological Factors Affecting Other Medical Conditions: Actual medical condition present; psychological/behavioral factors adversely affect course, treatment, risks, or pathophysiology. Severity: Mild to Extreme.\n\n5. Factitious Disorder: Intentional falsification or induction of disease with deception; motivation is internal (sick role); ABSENCE of external incentives. Imposed on self vs. Imposed on another (perpetrator receives diagnosis).\n\n6. Malingering: Intentional fabrication motivated by OBVIOUS EXTERNAL INCENTIVES (money, avoiding jail, drugs); V-code, not a mental disorder.",
        'specifiers' => "Review of essential specifiers: 'With predominant pain' in SSD; Care-seeking vs. Care-avoidant in IAD; Clinical subtypes in Conversion Disorder; Severity grading in PFAOMC; Imposed on self vs. Imposed on another in Factitious Disorder.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Key Board Differential Pearls:\n• Back pain for 8 months with panic that it's fatal and quitting job -> Somatic Symptom Disorder (symptoms present).\n• Terrified of having colon cancer for 8 months with ZERO physical symptoms -> Illness Anxiety Disorder (symptoms absent).\n• Sudden leg paralysis; paretic heel presses down when flexing normal hip -> Conversion Disorder (positive Hoover's sign).\n• Asthma patient refusing inhalers resulting in ICU intubation -> Psychological Factors Affecting Other Medical Conditions.\n• Injecting bacteria into IV line with no financial motive -> Factitious Disorder Imposed on Self.\n• Faking whiplash to claim $100,000 insurance settlement -> Malingering (external incentive present).\n• Mother poisoning child with salt -> Factitious Disorder Imposed on Another (mother diagnosed, not child).",
        'clinical_example' => "A comprehensive clinical review scenario: A board examination vignette describes a 30-year-old patient who presents with sudden bilateral blindness. When an optokinetic drum is rotated in front of the patient's eyes, optokinetic nystagmus is clearly observed. The patient appears surprisingly unconcerned about being blind (la belle indifférence).\n\nBoard Exam Question Formulation: What is the diagnosis? The candidate recognizes that the presence of optokinetic nystagmus provides positive clinical evidence of incompatibility with neurological blindness, confirming Conversion Disorder (Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder), With special sensory symptom.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board pearls summary: SSD = distressing somatic symptoms present + disproportionate thoughts/anxiety (>6 mos). IAD = health preoccupation with minimal/absent physical symptoms. Conversion = neurological symptom + positive incompatibility (Hoover's sign). PFAOMC = actual medical condition worsened by psychology. Factitious = feigned illness for internal sick role (NO external gain). Malingering = feigned illness for external incentives (money, avoiding jail). First-line management for SSD = scheduled visits with one primary care provider.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Somatic Diagnostic Decision Matrix', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Comprehensive command of duration thresholds, symptom counts, and severity rules across all somatic disorders.'],
            ['title' => 'The SSD vs. IAD Physical Threshold', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Physical symptoms present = SSD; physical symptoms absent/minimal = Illness Anxiety Disorder.'],
            ['title' => 'The Hoover Sign Benchmark', 'icon' => '🦶', 'definition' => 'Demonstrating involuntary motor extension proving functional neurological incompatibility in conversion paralysis.'],
            ['title' => 'The External Incentive Razor', 'icon' => '💰', 'definition' => 'Separating Factitious Disorder (internal sick role) from Malingering (external financial/legal incentive).']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Somatic Symptom Spectrum', 'definition' => 'The clinical continuum of conditions characterized by prominent physical complaints and associated psychological distress.'],
            ['term' => 'Functional Neurological Disorder', 'definition' => 'The preferred contemporary clinical term for Conversion Disorder, emphasizing physiological dysfunction over structural brain lesions.'],
            ['term' => 'Hoover\'s Sign', 'definition' => 'The classic bedside diagnostic maneuver proving intact involuntary motor pathways in functional leg weakness.'],
            ['term' => 'Factitious Deception', 'definition' => 'The deliberate falsification or induction of medical signs and symptoms in the absence of external rewards.']
        ]
    ]
];
